CROSS-DOMAIN PRINCIPLES SUPPORTED SYNTHETICALLY

1.
Surprise should first reduce confidence.

2.
Surprise magnitude and surprise identity are different variables.

3.
Copies are not redundancy.

4.
Physical cell identity is not causal-source independence.

5.
Useful redundancy requires partially independent error modes.

6.
Prediction quality is not causal-control validity.

7.
Missing or stale evidence is not negative evidence.

8.
Adaptation requires retirement / de-specialization.

9.
Confidence should modulate plasticity magnitude and evidence collection.

10.
Evidence itself is a finite adaptive resource.

11.
Evidence allocation should consider value and deadline,
not uncertainty alone.

12.
Inheritance should operate on a slower persistence timescale than fast role adaptation.

13.
Inherited state should bias fast behavior,
not override current utility.

14.
Short-lived adaptation should not automatically write heredity.

15.
Slow write and stale-prior suppression are separate problems.

16.
Stale-prior suppression and slow erase / rewrite should also be separate timescales.

17.
Retaining old memory and consolidating new memory form a real tradeoff.

